description:
Bring lessons 4.01 and 4.05 together into a small patching tool for builds of your own programs: patches written in a text file, located by offset or by a byte pattern so they survive rebuilds, checked before anything is written, and applied all together or not at all.

The patch file looks like this:

```text
# patches for Inventory.exe
[skip-intro]
offset: 0x1A40
original: 74 0C
replacement: EB 0C

[no-cooldown]
pattern: 2B C3 89 47 ?? 48 8B 0D
at: 2
original: 89 47 1C
replacement: 90 90 90
```

```cpp
struct PatchSpec
{
    std::string name;
    std::optional<std::size_t> offset;
    std::string pattern;
    std::size_t at{ 0 };
    std::vector<std::uint8_t> original, replacement;
};

class PatchSet
{
public:
    bool parse(const std::string& text, std::string& error);
    const std::vector<PatchSpec>& patches() const;
    std::optional<std::size_t> locate(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& code, const PatchSpec& p) const;
    std::vector<std::string> check(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& code) const;
    bool applyAll(std::vector<std::uint8_t>& code) const;
    bool revertAll(std::vector<std::uint8_t>& code) const;
};
```

**parse** reads the file line by line (blank lines and lines starting with `#` are ignored; spaces around keys and values are trimmed). `[name]` starts a patch. Keys: `offset` (hex with `0x`, or decimal), `pattern` (lesson 4.05's syntax), `at` (decimal, default 0), `original` and `replacement` (hex bytes). On a problem it returns false with `error` set:
- `line N: unknown key` for a key it does not know, and `line N: key outside a patch` for a key before the first `[name]`;
- `line N: bad bytes` for an `original` or `replacement` that is not all two-digit hex bytes;
- `patch NAME: needs offset or pattern` when a patch has neither, or both;
- `patch NAME: original and replacement differ` when they are empty or not the same length.

**locate** returns the patch's offset: its `offset`, or for a pattern, the position of its **only** match plus `at`. No match or more than one match gives `std::nullopt`.

**check** returns the problems that would stop `applyAll`, in this order, each patch in file order: `NAME: not found` (cannot locate), `NAME: original bytes differ` (the bytes there are not `original`, or would run past the end), then for each pair of located patches whose byte ranges overlap, `A and B overlap` (A before B in the file).

**applyAll** writes every replacement if `check` finds nothing, and returns whether it did. **revertAll** puts back every `original`, but only if every patch currently holds its `replacement`; otherwise it changes nothing and returns false.

